T:What it is
T:Probability
T:concerns
T:events and
T:numerical
T:descriptions
T:of how
T:likely they
T:are to
T:occur. The
T:probability
T:of an event
T:is a number
T:between 0
T:and 1; the
T:larger the
T:probability,
T:the more
T:likely an
T:event is to
T:occur. This
T:number is
T:often
T:expressed as
T:a percentage
T:(%), ranging
T:from 0% to
T:100%. A
T:simple
T:example is
T:the tossing
T:of a fair
T:(unbiased)
T:coin. Since
T:the coin is
T:fair, the
T:two outcomes
T:are both
T:equally
T:probable;
T:the
T:probability
T:of "heads"
T:equals the
T:probability
T:of "tails";
T:and since no
T:other
T:outcomes are
T:possible,
T:the
T:probability
T:of either
T:"heads" or
T:"tails" is
T:1/2.
